Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic drama, naturalistic faces, period fabrics, winter fog, and candlelit interiors.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Ebenezer Scrooge, a lean elderly man with a pointed nose, red-rimmed eyes, wiry white hair, and a black frock coat, hunches over ledgers beside a meagre coal fire.

(SOUND: wind rattles the windowpanes, pens scratch, distant church bells)

Bob Cratchit, a slight young clerk in a worn brown coat and long white comforter, copies letters in the freezing clerk's tank.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

Bob involuntarily claps once, then pokes his tiny fire until its last spark dies.

(SOUND: poker scrapes the grate; the office clock ticks)

FRED
Christmas is a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time, when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
Come and dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

Fred smiles sadly toward Bob and leaves through the fogged doorway.

Two portly charity collectors wait with papers and hats in hand. Scrooge waves them away before they can speak.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
“Many can't go there; and many would rather die.”

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

The collectors withdraw. Scrooge buttons his coat to the chin while Bob reaches for his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
Be here all the earlier next morning.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bleak chamber holds a narrow bed, a low fire, Dutch biblical tiles, a basin of gruel, and a massive door with heavy bolts.

(SOUND: the front door slams below; echoes roll through the stairwell)

Scrooge, now in a black dressing-gown and nightcap, studies the fireplace tiles. Every painted face seems to become Jacob Marley's.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

The unused bell above him begins to sway.

(SOUND: one bell trembles, then every bell in the house rings)

A chain drags up from the cellar, clanking step by step toward the door.

(SOUND: iron chain clanks over stone; a low musical drone rises)

The door opens without moving its bolts. Jacob Marley enters: transparent, spectacled, in his old waistcoat and pigtail, wrapped in a chain of cash-boxes, keys, ledgers, padlocks, and purses.

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese.

Marley tears the bandage from his jaw. It drops upon his breast.

(SOUND: Marley's jaw cracks open; the chain crashes against the floor)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

MARLEY
Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were all my business.

SCROOGE
Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Expect the first when the bell tolls One.

Marley backs toward the window. Beyond it, chained phantoms drift through the black sky above London.

(SOUND: spectral voices moan in the wind; the window shutters bang)

MARLEY
For your own sake, remember what has passed between us!

Marley floats out. Scrooge shuts the window, double-checks the bolts, and falls fully clothed across the bed.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Frost whitens the window. Scrooge wakes under drawn curtains as a church clock approaches one.

(SOUND: clockwork ticks loudly; a deep bell tolls one)

A brilliant light floods the room. The Ghost of Christmas Past stands beside the bed: child-sized yet ancient, white-haired, bare-footed, in a white tunic with holly and summer flowers, light streaming from its crown.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brought you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this.

The Spirit touches Scrooge's heart. The bedchamber wall dissolves into white winter light.

(SOUND: wind swells into children's distant laughter)

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD OUTSIDE THE BOARDING-SCHOOL - DAY

Snow lies over hedges and fields. A younger Scrooge, thin and dark-haired in an oversized school coat, watches happy boys race home in carts and on ponies.

(SOUND: pony hooves, boys shouting, sleigh bells)

The elderly Scrooge stares at his younger self through tears.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. BOARDING-SCHOOL CLASSROOM - DAY

Bare desks stretch beneath cracked windows. Young Scrooge reads alone by a feeble fire while imagined storybook figures shimmer at the edge of his book.

(SOUND: mice scuffle in the walls; thaw water drips outside)

SCROOGE
Why, it’s Ali Baba! Dear old honest Ali Baba!

Young Fan rushes in, rosy-cheeked and small, wearing a blue wool cloak and bonnet. She throws her arms around Young Scrooge.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother!

YOUNG SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever!

The older Scrooge presses a hand to his mouth as Fan pulls her brother toward the waiting coach.

(SOUND: carriage wheels crunch over snow; Fan's laughter fades)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child. Your nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Warm lamps transform the warehouse. Young Scrooge, now a slim apprentice with neatly combed dark hair and a plain waistcoat, helps Dick Wilkins raise the shutters.

(SOUND: shutters clatter upward; a fiddle tunes brightly)

Fezziwig, a round, silver-haired employer in a Welsh wig and generous burgundy waistcoat, claps his hands from behind a high desk.

FEZZIWIG
No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer!

The room clears. A fiddler plays as Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig dance amid apprentices and guests.

(SOUND: fiddle races through “Sir Roger de Coverley”; dancers stamp and laugh)

The elderly Scrooge watches Young Scrooge whirl happily with the crowd.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

SCROOGE
He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil.

The dance slows. Young Scrooge turns down the lamps, and the music falls away.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

A modest parlour glows with firelight. Belle, a graceful young woman in a simple mourning dress, faces Young Scrooge across a small table.

(SOUND: firewood settles; rain taps softly against the window)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty.

BELLE
You fear the world too much. Your nobler aspirations have fallen off one by one, until Gain engrosses you.

Young Scrooge looks away, fingers tightening around a coin.

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

The parlour shifts to Belle's later home. Belle, now a warm-faced mother, sits by a fire while children tumble around her husband.

(SOUND: children shriek with laughter; a front door opens)

BELLE'S HUSBAND
I passed his office window. His partner lies upon the point of death, and there he sat alone.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

Scrooge wrestles the Spirit's extinguisher-cap over its radiant head.

(SOUND: a high ringing tone dims beneath the cap; Scrooge's breath catches)

SCROOGE
Leave me! Take me back. Haunt me no longer!

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes in his bed as ruddy light pours from the adjoining room.

(SOUND: the clock tolls one; a roaring fire crackles nearby)

He opens the door. His own chamber has become a green bower piled with fruit, steaming punch, geese, puddings, and a gigantic Christmas feast.

The Ghost of Christmas Present sits among it all: a towering, broad-chested man with long dark curls, a holly wreath, a green fur-trimmed robe, and a glowing torch.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! And know me better, man!

SCROOGE
I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now.

SCROOGE
To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

Snow is scraped from shopfronts. Bakers, grocers, and families bustle past bright windows carrying dinners toward ovens.

(SOUND: shovels scrape pavement, carts rumble, church bells ring)

The Spirit scatters sparks from his torch over quarrelling dinner-carriers; their scowls become smiles.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - CHRISTMAS DAY

A small four-room home glows around a crowded table. Mrs. Cratchit, brisk and loving in a twice-turned green gown with bright ribbons, tends a goose.

(SOUND: potatoes bubble beneath a saucepan lid; children chatter)

Martha slips in from work. Peter, tall for his age in Bob's oversized shirt collar, pokes the fire.

Bob enters in his threadbare brown coat and long white comforter, carrying Tiny Tim. Tim is pale and delicate, with curly brown hair, a little crutch, and iron supports on his legs.

(SOUND: Tiny Tim's crutch taps the floor; the family cheers)

BOB CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

MRS. CRATCHIT
As good as gold?

BOB CRATCHIT
He told me he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple.

BOB CRATCHIT
It might be pleasant to them to remember who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The goose arrives at table. The family watches Mrs. Cratchit carve it with solemn anticipation.

(SOUND: carving knife cuts crisp skin; delighted voices rise)

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

The family lifts small cups of hot drink beside the fire.

BOB CRATCHIT
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

SCROOGE
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner.

SCROOGE
No, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

The Spirit turns its grave face toward Scrooge.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows his head. Bob raises a reluctant toast.

BOB CRATCHIT
I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear. The children! Christmas Day.

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - NIGHT

Fred, ruddy and handsome in a dark green waistcoat, laughs beside his pretty wife in a crimson dress and several friends gathered around a fire.

(SOUND: glasses clink; laughter spills over a harp melody)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it. He don't make himself comfortable with it.

FRED
I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him.

The unseen Scrooge tries to answer their game, delighted despite himself.

(SOUND: a blind-man's-buff player bumps a chair; everyone laughs louder)

FRED
Here is a glass of mulled wine ready to our hand. Uncle Scrooge!

FRED'S WIFE
Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge lifts an invisible glass, smiling.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas and a happy New Year to you all.

EXT. LONDON EXCHANGE - NIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present has vanished. A tall, silent figure in a deep black hood glides through icy darkness, one hand extended.

(SOUND: midnight bells fade; wind moans through the exchange archway)

Scrooge follows the Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come past merchants in black coats.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen.

SCROOGE
Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me.

Three businessmen murmur together, indifferent.

(SOUND: coins chink in pockets; a watch case snaps shut)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
“I only know he's dead.”

NARRATOR (V.O.)
“It's likely to be a very cheap funeral.”

The hooded Spirit points onward. Scrooge looks for his own familiar office window, but another man occupies his chair.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT

Rusty keys, chains, rags, bottles, and bones crowd a low, smoky room. Old Joe, grey-haired and sharp-eyed, sits by a charcoal stove.

(SOUND: rain hisses outside; Joe's pipe crackles)

Mrs. Dilber, a hard-faced charwoman in a patched shawl, drops bed-curtains and silver onto the floor. A laundress and undertaker's assistant add smaller bundles.

MRS. DILBER
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime?

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

MRS. DILBER
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say.

Joe counts coins into separate piles. The women laugh.

(SOUND: coins clatter on bare boards; a distant cat yowls)

MRS. DILBER
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

Scrooge recoils as the Spirit reveals a bare bed in a dark room. A covered corpse lies alone beneath a ragged sheet.

(SOUND: rats gnaw beneath the hearthstone; a door creaks in the empty house)

SCROOGE
Spirit! The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

The same small home is quiet. Bob, still slight in his worn brown coat and white comforter, enters slowly through the door. Tiny Tim's empty stool stands by the hearth.

(SOUND: a clock ticks; sewing needles pass through cloth)

Mrs. Cratchit lowers her work. Peter reads from a book while the younger children sit still.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child!

Bob climbs to the room above, where Tiny Tim's crutch rests beside a small chair.

(SOUND: Bob's slow footsteps on the stairs; a single floorboard creaks)

He kisses the child's unseen face and returns to his family.

BOB CRATCHIT
When we recollect how patient and how mild he was, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Never, father.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Weeds choke a churchyard hemmed in by black houses. The Ghost stands beside a neglected grave, its hand pointing down.

(SOUND: winter wind whistles through iron gates; a raven beats its wings)

Scrooge approaches the stone and brushes away wet leaves. The name beneath is EBENEZER SCROOGE.

(SOUND: a low funeral bell tolls once; music becomes a thin sustained chord)

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge and back again.

SCROOGE
Are these the shadows of things that Will be, or shadows of things that May be, only?

The hood remains still.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year.

SCROOGE
Tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

Scrooge clutches the Spirit's hand. The black hood collapses into the bedpost of his own bed.

(SOUND: the bell cuts off; Scrooge gasps awake)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Sunlight floods the familiar room. Scrooge leaps from bed, dressed in his black nightcap and gown, laughing through tears.

(SOUND: jubilant church bells ring across clear London air)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me!

He flings open the window. A young errand boy passes below, but Scrooge calls to him without waiting for an answer.

SCROOGE
Go and buy the big prize Turkey, and tell 'em to bring it here!

Scrooge dresses himself in his best black frock coat, white shirt, and polished boots. He writes Bob's address with a shaking hand.

(SOUND: pen scratches rapidly; Scrooge laughs)

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

Scrooge strides through sunlit snow, smiling at passersby. He stops the portly charity collector from the previous day and takes both his hands.

(SOUND: cheerful foot traffic, carol singers, bells)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? A merry Christmas to you, sir!

The collector stares as Scrooge whispers an amount in his ear.

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

The collector grasps Scrooge's hands, overwhelmed. Scrooge continues toward Fred's house.

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - CHRISTMAS DAY

The table is laid for dinner. Fred and his wife look up as Scrooge cautiously opens the door.

(SOUND: cutlery settles; a fire crackles warmly)

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred rushes forward and embraces him.

(SOUND: chairs scrape back; delighted laughter and applause)

FRED
Let him in? Of course!

Scrooge joins the family at the Christmas table, his severe face softened by a broad, unpractised smile.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - MORNING

The office is bright with a large new fire. Scrooge, in his black frock coat, waits behind his desk with the door wide open.

(SOUND: coal crackles warmly; the clock strikes nine)

Bob hurries in eighteen minutes late, still thin, still wearing his worn brown coat and white comforter.

SCROOGE
What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob edges toward the desk, eyeing the ruler.

(SOUND: Bob's boots shuffle; the ruler taps the desktop)

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

Scrooge steps forward and grips Bob's shoulder.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB CRATCHIT
Sir?

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family.

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob stares, then laughs in disbelief. Scrooge opens the coal-box himself and pours coal onto Bob's fire.

(SOUND: coal tumbles into the grate; the fire blooms)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.
